In reading people's complaints about some earlier ideas, I have come
up with a few ideas that I think don't fall into the usual traps.

1) Genocided monsters don't show up in the regular game, but do show
up in the planes, and show up with increased frequency in Gehennom.

Pros: 
This forces the player to deal with challenges in the endgame that
they might otherwise not have to worry about.
It makes for an interesting tactical decision: I could genocide L now
to make the game easier until I am stronger, but that will make the
endgame harder.
It makes sense; Gehennom is where dead things go, and genocided
species lead to a lot of dead things of one type.

Cons: 
Genoless is already one of the easier conducts, so this doesn't make
the game much harder.  Although I suppose somone who really wants a
challenge could purposefully genocide hard monsters in the Valley.
Also, this would make scrolls of genocide not worth keeping in the
planes.

2) You are limited in what you can carry to Gehennom, and then again
in what you can carry to the Planes.  This could be both in terms of
types of items and numbers.  Perhaps no wands of wishing or containers
can go.  Perhaps you are limited to 5 items to Gehennom, and 9 in the
planes. Or the limit could apply only to magic items.  Or you could
assign items a value and limit the total value.  You would want to
mark items that have been carried through with an attunement bit (the
equivalent of an amusement park hand stamp) to get them back in.  Hide
this bit from the player so they can't wish for it.  The invocation
artifacts, your OWN quest artifact, and any items generated in
Gehennom would come pre-stamped.  Explain it with a magic portal
leading from the Valley to Gehennom proper.  Transporting too much
magic is beyond its power.

Pros:
You can't just build an invincible kit once, and have the game all but
done.
You have a tactical choice to make.  Which items do you really need?
Finding powerful items is half the fun, so if you get to do it more,
that's more fun, right?

Cons:
This would require a whole lot of coding and break save compatibility.
Tweaking the balance of exactly which items to limit would be very
tricky; you don't to leave the game too easy, but you also don't want
to make the game too dependant on luck right after the start of
Gehennom.
Unless explained by some sort of Deus ex Machina, this is going to be
very confusing to unspoiled players.

3) Make some monsters more difficult for a better equipped player.  A
doppleganger that gets all the equipment you have (but drops none of
it).  A forsaker that does more damage the more magical AC you have,
and takes less damage from enchanted weapons.  A monster that reverses
your natural regeneration for a time.  A monster whose nastiest attack
only works on characters with reflection (a harmonic golem).  A monk
that does more damage the more strength you have.  I'm sure there are
more possibilities.  These monsters should be non-genocidable, and
only appear in Gehennom, or be combined with option 1.

Pros:
Who doesn't want more monsters with interesting abilities?
Tactical decisions on whether to use certain equipment, or get more of
certain stats.  Is the benefit worth making certain monsters much
tougher?

Cons:
Might just lead to tedious inventory management.
Probably breaks save compatibility.
These types of monsters are more often the domain of SLASH'EM.

